query:
I have taken photos only with a phone for years, but my indoor volleyball shots often look blurred or noisy. I have about $700 for my first dedicated camera. I am tempted to choose between an older interchangeable-lens camera with a larger sensor and a newer compact with a smaller sensor, but the budget and my phone symptoms are only starting anchors, not measured results.

The frozen shopping set contains an Olympus E-M1 body page at $668, a Panasonic ZS70 fixed-zoom bundle at $379.08, a Panasonic LX10 one-inch fixed-lens bundle at $514.87, a Canon 80D body page at $798, a Micro Four Thirds 35-100mm f/2.8 lens page at $897.99 and a Canon-compatible 50-100mm f/1.8 lens page at $999. Frozen model context places the E-M1 in 2013 with a Four Thirds sensor and interchangeable mount, the LX10 in 2016 with a 13.2 by 8.8mm sensor and fixed 24-72mm-equivalent f/1.4-f/2.8 lens, the 80D in 2016 as a DSLR, and the ZS70 row in 2017 with a 1/2.3-type sensor and fixed 24-720mm-equivalent f/3.3-f/6.4 lens. Background material covers sensor format, aperture, exposure time, motion blur, image noise, autofocus and sports-photography equipment. Three community pages contain only limited questions or individual reports about dark-condition settings and phone noise, focus or oversharpening. Nothing in the packet gives a controlled same-gym comparison, verified current condition and delivered cost, a complete compatible system under $700, or a universal sensor-size or release-year winner.

Please audit what these materials can and cannot establish and give me a practical first-camera recommendation process. Use all of these boundaries:

- keep all SKU price stock rating review bundle warranty feature and performance wording at frozen seller assertion scope;
- bind every offer to the exact model body or fixed lens form mount bundle and page condition;
- keep the 700 dollar figure as an unverified budget anchor until current tax shipping lens and accessory costs are known;
- do not compare body only and fixed lens bundle prices as if both were complete system costs;
- verify exact current model condition shutter count battery mount lens aperture reach AF burst buffer card warranty return and delivered cost fields;
- include a compatible sport appropriate lens and required accessories in the total cost of each interchangeable lens option;
- preserve exact release sensor format and lens form mappings without turning age sensor area megapixels or zoom into a universal rank;
- separate field of view and equivalent focal length from light gathering, focus behavior and image quality;
- explain the joint shutter aperture ISO noise AF tracking burst reach position and technique constraints for motion in dim light;
- do not treat stabilization as proof that subject motion will be frozen;
- keep each forum statement at its author device sample question and time scope;
- compare candidates only under the same gym position action output size and declared setting strategy;
- record motion freeze focus hit noise detail keeper rate handling failures and total cost as separate outcomes;
- predeclare identity condition compatibility reach aperture AF burst output handling and budget pass fail stops;
- do not allow release year sensor format megapixels zoom store rating or one lucky frame to override a failed complete system gate; and
- choose only the lowest total cost exact passing system otherwise rent borrow test keep the phone save or defer.

Compare these approaches instead of assuming one is correct:

1. buy the Olympus body because its older larger sensor must beat every newer compact;
2. buy the ZS70 because 2017 30x zoom and a 95 percent store rating must win indoor volleyball;
3. buy the LX10 because one inch f 1 4 and a 514 87 dollar bundle must guarantee reach tracking and keeper rate;
4. buy the Canon 80D body even though its frozen body price exceeds the budget and ignore lens cost;
5. attach any fast telephoto to any body without mount or total cost checks;
6. rank the options by release year sensor size megapixels zoom or one forum story without a same gym trial; or
7. choose the lowest total cost exact passing system otherwise rent borrow test keep the phone save or defer.

First, build an exact-system table that separates literal frozen seller fields, independently mapped model facts and unresolved fields. Include body or fixed-lens status, sensor format, release timing, mount, lens focal range and aperture, frozen price and rating, exact condition unknowns, required accessories and a transparent complete delivered-cost calculation against the $700 anchor.

Second, explain how sensor format and release year interact with field of view, aperture, exposure time, motion blur, ISO or gain, noise, autofocus, continuous shooting, buffer behavior, lens reach, photographer position and technique. Be explicit about which general mechanisms only identify what must be tested and which claims would require measurements from these exact units in my gym.

Third, design the smallest practical repeated comparison. Specify how I should inspect, rent, borrow or conduct a return-compliant trial; keep gym, position, volleyball action and final output size matched; choose and record shutter, aperture and ISO strategies; repeat enough bursts; and measure motion-freeze rate, focus-hit rate, noise and retained detail, keeper rate, handling failures and total system cost separately. State every exact-unit, condition, compatibility, warranty and return check, and predeclare pass, fail and stop thresholds before I see which option looks best.

Finally, mark every candidate gate pass, fail or unresolved. Recommend only the lowest-total-cost exact system that clears all identity, condition, compatibility, reach, aperture, autofocus, burst, same-gym output, handling and budget gates. If none clears them, say whether I should rent or borrow for a corrected trial, keep using the phone while improving position and settings, save for a complete system, or defer. Scope the conclusion to my tested gym, actions, output size, budget and exact units rather than claiming a universal rule about sensor size or release year.
